  <h2 id="what">What this site is</h2>
  <p>Computer Science Guide explains the fundamentals that sit underneath everyday programming: complexity analysis, data structures, algorithms, object-oriented design, how the .NET runtime behaves, relational databases, networking, operating systems, version control and testing.</p>
  <p>It is aimed at people who already write code, often self-taught or a few years into a job, and who want to understand why the tools they use behave the way they do. Examples are in C#, because that is the language the author works in, and because most material on these subjects is written for Python, Java or pseudocode and never connects the theory to .NET types such as <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Dictionary&lt;TKey,TValue&gt;</code> or <code>Task</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="author">Who writes it</h2>
  <p>The site is written and maintained by one person, publishing as <strong>Computer Science Guide</strong> for now. A named byline and bio are coming.</p>
  <p>There are no guest posts, no sponsored articles and no anonymous staff writers. The same author is responsible for every article's accuracy.</p>

  <h2 id="how">How articles are made</h2>
  <p>The full process is in the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>. In short:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Articles are researched from primary sources: language and protocol specifications, official documentation, source code and standard textbooks. Each article lists its sources with the date they were consulted.</li>
    <li>Every C#, SQL and shell example is executed by a verification tool. If a program does not compile, or prints something different from what the article shows, the article cannot be published.</li>
    <li>Drafting is assisted by AI tools, and the author reviews each article before it is published. The editorial policy explains exactly where AI is and is not used.</li>
    <li>Mistakes are corrected openly. Substantive fixes are recorded on the <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="history">History of the site</h2>
  <p>An earlier version of this site published short overview guides on a wide range of subjects, including web development, React and interview preparation. In 2026 it was rebuilt around fewer subjects covered in more depth, and those older guides were retired. Their addresses redirect to the closest current topic.</p>

  <h2 id="funding">Funding and independence</h2>
  <p>The site is free to read and has no paywall, accounts or newsletter. It does not currently show advertising, contains no affiliate links, and does not accept payment for coverage or links. If advertising is introduced, the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> will be updated before it appears, and advertisers will have no say over what is written.</p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions, corrections and suggestions are welcome through the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a>.</p>
